\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\title{The Wilson term and other doubling remedies}
\date{2026-11-01}

\p{Can the extra zero branches of the centered Dirac operator
be lifted without abandoning a local lattice difference?
The [covariant operator](fdip-009N) supplies a skew-adjoint
first difference. A second difference supplies the Wilson term.
For a positive Wilson coefficient and trivial connection, its
nonnegative scalar Fourier symbol vanishes only at zero momentum.
Adding it to the skew Dirac symbol therefore leaves precisely the
constant zero modes.}

\p{The factor of lattice spacing and the adjoint convention are
part of this statement. On one direction the free skew block
satisfies #{D_1(p)^2=-\sin^2 p\,\bbI}. That direction supplies
no unit bivector of a plane with square #{-1}, so the square is
the real statement and #{\ui D_1} is the Hermitian comparison.
Replacing the skew operator by #{\ui D_1} changes the kernel
calculation and can create extra zeros (\ref{fdip-009W}).
\ref{fdip-00LZ} separates that
replacement from the Clifford substitution #{\varepsilon_j^{S}\mapsto\ui\varepsilon_j^{S}}.
The calculation below uses the already specified
reducible Clifford module and internal fiber; it does not identify
one surviving momentum block with a minimal physical spinor.}

\p{What does the Wilson term cost in chirality, and which remedies
keep it? Suppose a site-independent self-adjoint spin grading
anticommutes with every Clifford generator. Acting by the identity
on the internal fiber, it commutes with every link transport.
The centered operator is odd for this grading. Each generator
exchanges the two grading spaces, so they have equal dimensions.
A normal operator odd for the grading then has equally many zero
modes in both spaces. The volume grading is an example in positive
even dimension.}

\p{For a free Clifford symbol whose vector part has
nondegenerate vertex zeros, their local degrees and the aggregate
off-grid contribution on the interpolated momentum grid sum to
zero. With positive Wilson coefficient the free scalar mass
breaks anticommutation and lifts the zero blocks at nonzero
sampled corners, leaving the vector part unchanged. A complex
Ginsparg–Wilson operator #{D} can have nonzero graded kernel index.
Its modified chiral rotation by any nonzero complex scalar #{u}
fixes the bilinear. The inverse pullback multiplies the
coefficient functional by #{u^{-2\,\sdim\ker D}}. On even tori the
centered operator reduces to staggered fields for arbitrary
unitary links. For based cochains with trivial links and a nonzero
finite-dimensional constant complex Hermitian fiber, take the
counting adjoint of the coboundary. Then #{\extd+\extdag} is
unitarily equivalent to #{2\ui} times the full one-component
staggered difference on the doubled torus at unit fine spacing.
The cochain kernels are compared in
[Dirac–Kähler operators](fdip-0091).}

